 "lane": "native-resolution LiDAR scarp channels",
 "hypothesis": "A slope-aware antislope scarplet-template response combined with Steger/Frangi multiscale line evidence from the 2 m GeoDAWN lidar stack, used as an emission intensity rather than a ranking, will place dots on catalogue faults that were withheld from training better than mass-matched random placement and better than catalogue-distance alone.",
 "mechanism": "A tectonic scarp step is a coherent, along-strike persistent, uphill-facing (antislope) monoclinal step; fluvial terrace risers, channel banks and field edges produce predominantly downslope- or cross-slope-facing steps. Subtracting the strongest competing step orientation from the antislope member isolates the tectonic-favouring signature, and the Hessian line measures reward along-strike persistence across scales.",
 "named_non_fault_mimic": "Fluvial terrace riser / alluvial-fan head incision. The same signature is also produced by channel banks (cross_max), landslide headscarps, paleo-shorelines, and unmapped roads, canals and field edges; no cultural-feature mask was present in the restored inputs (see open_irregularities).",
